// §comptime and §generics — types are values, and a generic is a function.
//
// **All of this parses, and none of it runs.** As of 2026-09-07 the spellings
// are all real: `comptime` is a token in both of its positions, `struct { … }`
// is an expression, a call is a type, and `Type` is a predeclared name holding
// the type of types. A type is an ordinary value that a `let` can bind and an
// annotation can name — so the lines below that only need *that* much already
// work, and `let Coord = …` is a binding like any other.
//
// What is missing is the evaluation. `Pair(u64, Str)` has to be *called* at
// compile time, which needs the comptime configuration of the evaluator and a
// cache keyed on `(declaration, comptime arguments)`, and neither exists. So
// elaboration answers with "comptime is not supported yet" and "generic
// instantiation is not supported yet", and the errors downstream of those —
// `T` not in scope, arguments that are types where runtime values are wanted —
// are that one gap seen from further along.
//
// Nothing here reaches core IR. `Type` has no runtime representation, and
// every position that would put one in a slot, a global, a field, a parameter,
// or a return is refused — which is how "core IR is free of comptime,
// generics, and `Type`" stays true while the front of the pipeline learns to
// talk about them.
//
// It is here as a target. §comptime decided this design on 2026-08-18 and
// implemented none of it, and the gap between those two facts is the widest in
// the checklist — so the decision is written out as programs, where it is
// easier to notice that something is wrong with it.
//
// The one thing to know before reading: §generics has no syntax. There are no
// angle brackets in this file and no turbofish, because a generic is a
// function that takes a type and returns one, and an instantiation is a call.
// §expressions' postfix rung already parses every line of it.

// ---- A generic type is a function that returns one -------------------------

// The conventional spelling on the left is sugar for the one on the right
// (§comptime, jointly with §types). Both declare the same thing, and a reader
// who never needs to know that a type is a value never has to see the second.
//
//   struct Point { x: s64, y: s64 }   ≡   let Point = struct { x: s64, y: s64 };
//   type InstanceId = u64;            ≡   let InstanceId = u64;
//
// Once that is true, a generic needs nothing new. `Type` is the type whose
// values are types, and it is a predeclared name rather than a keyword — the
// same status `u64`, `bool`, and `Str` already have (§lexical).

fn Pair(comptime A: Type, comptime B: Type) -> Type {
  struct { first: A, second: B }
}

// The struct is anonymous because a generic has to return a type it cannot
// name in advance. That is the one thing §comptime adds to §types' repertoire
// of type-introducing forms.

fn Node(comptime T: Type) -> Type {
  struct { value: T, next: Node(T) }
}

// `Node` mentions itself, which under §types' reference semantics is an
// ordinary thing to write rather than an infinite size. It terminates because
// instantiation is memoized: the inner `Node(T)` hits the cache entry that the
// outer call is still filling in, exactly as `Types::fresh_struct` allocates a
// type before `set_fields` gives it fields.
//
// Worth being honest about: this type is *representable* and no finite value
// of it can be built. §types has no `nil` and §unions is unstarted, so there
// is nothing to put in `next` to end a list. A real linked list waits on
// §unions, not on this file.

// ---- Instantiation is a call -----------------------------------------------

// A type annotation is a type annotation. What is new is that the thing in it
// may be a call, evaluated before the annotation has a meaning.

fn area(p: Pair(s64, s64)) -> s64 {
  p.first * p.second
}

// `<` never has to be disambiguated, which is a rule C++ and Rust both spend
// and Glue does not have to. `a < b` is a comparison. Always. A generic
// instantiation is indistinguishable from any other call, which is why
// §generics' syntax slot is empty by construction rather than by omission.

// Because a type is a value, an instantiation can be bound like any other
// value, and the binding is a perfectly ordinary type name afterwards.

let Coord = Pair(s64, s64);
let here: Coord = Coord { first: 3, second: 4 };

// **Open, and not decided anywhere:** whether the head of a struct literal may
// itself be a call — `Pair(s64, s64) { first: 3, second: 4 }`. Today
// `struct_lit` expects a name there. Binding the instantiation first, as
// above, needs no new grammar and is what this file uses.

// ---- Identity comes from the cache -----------------------------------------

// Instantiations are memoized on `(declaration, comptime argument values)`, so
// two types are the same type when they are the same cache entry. §types'
// nominal rule survives without a new clause: a generic type's identity is its
// declaration plus its arguments.

fn label(p: Pair(u64, Str)) -> Str {
  p.second
}

fn relabel(p: Pair(u64, Str)) -> Str {
  label(p)                 // fine: one cache entry, so one type
}

fn flipped(p: Pair(Str, u64)) -> Str {
  label(p)                 // a type error: arguments are ordered
}

// This is *not* structural interning. Two `struct { first: u64, second: Str }`
// written out by hand are two types, and must be — §types says every
// evaluation of a `struct { … }` expression produces a fresh one. `Pair(u64,
// Str)` is one type because the cache runs the body once, not because two
// identical shapes were merged.

// ---- A comptime argument need not be a type --------------------------------

// The payoff for refusing to make generics their own mechanism. The cache is
// keyed on comptime *values*, so a type parameter and a value parameter are
// the same thing and neither needs a feature the other does not.

fn Fixed(comptime T: Type, comptime capacity: u64) -> Type {
  struct { items: T, len: u64 }
}

fn used(f: Fixed(u64, 8)) -> u64 {
  f.len
}

fn spare(f: Fixed(u64, 8)) -> u64 {
  8u64 - used(f)           // fine: the same two arguments, so the same type
}

fn overflowing(f: Fixed(u64, 4096)) -> u64 {
  used(f)                  // a type error: a different `capacity`, so a
}                          // different cache entry, so a different type

// `Fixed(u64, 8)` and `Fixed(u64, 8)` are one type for the same reason
// `Pair(u64, Str)` is: one cache entry. No rule was added to say so, and
// nothing in the cache knows that one of those arguments was a type.

// ---- `comptime` also prefixes an expression --------------------------------

// The keyword's second position (§comptime — one keyword, two positions). It
// says this must be evaluated during compilation, and is an error when it
// cannot be.

fn checksum(n: u64) -> u64 {
  let mut acc = 0u64;
  let mut i = 0u64;
  while i < n {
    acc = acc + i * i;
    i = i + 1u64;
  }
  acc
}

let table = comptime checksum(256u64);

// There is no `comptime let` and no `comptime var`. A `let` is already
// comptime-known when its initializer is (§statements), so a binding form
// would name the same thing twice.
//
// Evaluation is bounded — a fuel budget in steps and a recursion depth cap,
// each exhausted as an ordinary diagnostic rather than a hang. That is not
// polish: the language server elaborates half-typed programs on every
// keystroke, and a half-typed program is the likeliest thing in the language
// to loop forever.
//
// And it is hermetic. Comptime sees pure computation over comptime-known
// values and the declarations of this file. No image, no namespace, no
// messages, no host, no filesystem, no clock, no randomness — which for
// Othismo is a deferral rather than a refusal, and is deferred because opening
// it later is additive and closing it later breaks programs.

// ---- A generic function is the same idea -----------------------------------

fn first(comptime T: Type, xs: Node(T)) -> T {
  xs.value
}

fn max(comptime T: Type, a: T, b: T) -> T {
  if a > b { a } else { b }
}

// `max` is **accepted as written**, and that is the model's known weakness
// rather than a bug. A generic body is type-checked when it is instantiated;
// before that it gets lexing, parsing, and name resolution and nothing more.
// Whether `>` exists on `T` is answered when someone calls it, and the
// diagnostic arrives at the call site rather than here.
//
// That is a direct tax on goal §living: a generic nobody has called yet cannot
// be given a red squiggle, and no amount of language-server effort changes it.
// Accepted, because the alternative — bounds strong enough to check a body
// ahead of instantiation — is a second type system layered on the first, and
// §generics has not established what a bound would be.

let biggest = max(u64, 3, 9);
let earlier = max(Str, "ant", "bee");

// The type argument is passed, not inferred. **Open (§inference):** whether it
// may be inferred at a call site, and what the explicit form is for when
// inference fails.

// ---- What is still open ----------------------------------------------------

// Written down so the file is not read as a complete design:
//
//  * Bounds and constraints — can a generic body use `+` on its `T` at all,
//    and what is the spelling (§generics, §objects).
//  * Variance for generic containers, or invariance only (§generics).
//  * Inference of type arguments at a call site (§inference).
//  * How a comptime function *rejects* its arguments. Without it, a bad
//    instantiation fails somewhere inside a body its caller did not write.
//  * `comptime var` and `inline for` — mutation during comptime evaluation,
//    and a loop that unrolls because its bound is comptime-known while its
//    body is not.
//  * Whether `Type` values support equality, ordering, or printing.
//  * Generic message handlers — Othismo-specific, and the hardest of these.
//    A handler's arguments arrive as BSON on the wire, and what a type
//    parameter means once a value has been serialized and routed is not
//    obviously anything (§modules, §errors).
